.TH SH-CIRCUIT 1
.SH NAME
circuit \- shell script interface to circuit(2)'s netlists
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B load circuit

.B id := ${circuit new}
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B spec
.I netlist-line...
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B read
.I file
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B dc
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B watch
.BR v | i
.I name
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.BR begin | begindc
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B step
.I dt
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B run
.I dt nsteps
.br
.B circuit
.I id
.B clear
.br
.B ${circuit
.I id
.BR node | current
.I name
.B }
.br
.B ${circuit
.I id
.BR trace | names | rows | columns | time | analysis | text
.B }
.br
.B circuit del
.I id...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Circuit
makes
.IR circuit (2)
usable from
.IR sh (1):
a netlist can be run and its answer looked at without writing a Limbo
program.
.PP
No netlist vocabulary is added here.
What
.B spec
accumulates and
.B read
loads is
.IR circuit (2)'s
own language, documented there, and every line is passed through
untouched.
.PP
.B Spec
appends one line; several calls build the netlist up, and a single
quoted argument containing newlines does it in one.
The netlist is parsed the first time something needs it, so there is no
.B commit
to remember.
.PP
.B Dc
solves the operating point.
.B Begin
sets the circuit at rest and
.B begindc
at the operating point; one of them must come before any stepping.
.B Step
takes one step and
.B run
takes several.
.PP
.B Watch
says what to record, and must be said before the run \- otherwise the
earlier rows would have fewer columns than the later ones, and it is
refused rather than silently ragged.
.B v
records a node's voltage and
.B i
a part's current.
.SS Plotting what happened
.B Trace
returns the whole recorded waveform as one flat list: the time and then
each watched value, for every row in turn.
That is exactly the shape
.IR sh-plot (1)'s
.B append
takes, so a transient analysis becomes a picture in one command:
.IP
.EX
plot $p history append ${circuit $c trace}
.EE
.PP
.B Names
gives the column headings in the same order,
.B rows
and
.B columns
their counts.
.SH EXAMPLE
A divider, and then the step response of an RC:
.IP
.EX
load std
load circuit
c := ${circuit new}
circuit $c spec 'V1 in 0 10
R1 in mid 1k
R2 mid 0 3k'
circuit $c dc
echo ${circuit $c node mid}		# 7.5

r := ${circuit new}
circuit $r spec 'V1 in 0 1
R1 in out 1k
C1 out 0 1u'
circuit $r watch v out
circuit $r watch i C1
circuit $r begin
circuit $r run 1e-4 5
echo ${circuit $r names}		# t out C1
echo ${circuit $r trace}
circuit del $c $r
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/sh/circuit.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sh (1),
.IR sh-plot (1),
.IR circuit (2),
.IR circuittest (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
A malformed netlist, a circuit with no single answer, a transient
stepped before its initial condition is set, or a
.B watch
added after recording has begun, each fail the command with the message
.IR circuit (2)
gives.
.SH BUGS
There is no test for this module in
.BR /appl/cmd .
.IR Circuit (2)
underneath it is checked thoroughly by
.IR circuittest (1),
and what is added here is handle bookkeeping and the flattening of the
trace, which is exercised only by the example above and by
.BR /lib/demo/circuitlab.sh .
.PP
A netlist cannot be changed once it has been parsed; a new handle is
needed, which is what the demo does on every slider move.
Rebuilding is cheap for the circuits this is for and wrong for a large
one.
.PP
.B Trace
returns every row as shell words, so a run of ten thousand steps builds
a list of tens of thousands of strings.
Record what is wanted and
.B clear
between runs.
